0.0230255126953125, 0.0263824462890625, 0.0225830078125, -0.03790283203125, 0.0439453125, 0.0008435249328613281, 0.004390716552734375, -0.039031982421875, -0.00893402099609375, 0.003734588623046875, -0.018280029296875, -0.00637054443359375, -0.03143310546875, 0.00881195068359375, -0.061187744140625, 0.0025424957275390625, -0.031585693359375, -0.00782012939453125, -0.002208709716796875, -0.041107177734375, -0.0245819091796875, -0.013641357421875, 0.026214599609375, -0.003551483154296875, 0.025177001953125, -0.01535797119140625, -0.010040283203125, -0.0095062255859375, -0.0399169921875, -0.02001953125, 0.0091705322265625, -0.00255584716796875, -0.0004897117614746094, -0.0194854736328125, 0.009307861328125, 0.032867431640625, -0.005062103271484375, -0.0142364501953125, -0.035186767578125, 0.0018358230590820312, 0.0026187896728515625, 0.005157470703125, -0.0293121337890625, 0.022735595703125, 0.00649261474609375, -0.0194549560546875, -0.00888824462890625, 0.00270843505859375, 0.00853729248046875, -0.0008788108825683594, 0.01605224609375, -0.0156402587890625, -0.002227783203125, -0.00908660888671875, 0.005435943603515625, -0.0217132568359375, -0.04656982421875, -0.01110076904296875, -0.0477294921875, -0.009979248046875, 0.0059814453125, 0.032745361328125, -0.00977325439453125, 0.00988006591796875, 0.0018472671508789062, -0.035369873046875, 0.027679443359375, -0.0249481201171875, 0.0189666748046875, -0.01348114013671875, 0.01055145263671875, 0.002216339111328125, 0.005001068115234375, 0.032745361328125, 0.028900146484375, -0.02642822265625, 0.0021953582763671875, -0.037109375, 0.0171966552734375, -0.004856109619140625, -0.00045180320739746094, 0.00408172607421875, 0.0267791748046875, 0.05767822265625, 0.0109710693359375, -0.026947021484375, -0.01470184326171875, 0.0005555152893066406, 0.003711700439453125, 0.01366424560546875, -0.01456451416015625, 0.0289764404296875, 0.0100860595703125, 0.00879669189453125, 0.0044403076171875, 0.0210418701171875, 0.0297088623046875, 0.0345458984375, -0.0200958251953125, -0.02215576171875, 0.004962921142578125, -0.0011625289916992188, 0.011932373046875, -0.0074005126953125, 0.007450103759765625, 0.003589630126953125, 0.003787994384765625, -0.0110931396484375, 0.0013141632080078125, 0.036529541015625, 0.0177154541015625, -0.004474639892578125, -0.026580810546875, 0.004268646240234375, -0.0254974365234375, 0.009857177734375, -0.01337432861328125, 0.0187530517578125, 0.0006222724914550781, -0.02264404296875, 0.0198516845703125, 0.006500244140625, 0.0161590576171875, -0.01059722900390625, 0.030242919921875, 0.0216522216796875, 0.00879669189453125, -0.017425537109375, 0.004207611083984375, 0.020416259765625, 0.0009169578552246094, 0.01094818115234375, 0.001312255859375, -0.0258941650390625, 0.006740570068359375, 0.0005311965942382812, -0.01177978515625, 0.01181793212890625, -0.005237579345703125, 0.0218963623046875, -0.05047607421875, 0.003688812255859375, -0.0231475830078125, -0.0316162109375, 0.00949859619140625, -0.03631591796875, 0.0010423660278320312, -0.00981903076171875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. This work is guided by the following research questions: (1) How robust are representative memory-based self-improving agent methods to stochasticity and task order? (2) What are the sources of fragility in agent memory banks, and how do they impact performance? (3) How does task and environment specification affect agent reliability? (4) What evaluation protocols and system design recommendations can mitigate observed fragility? \n\nTo address these questions, our main contributions are: (1) a systematic empirical analysis of the robustness of two representative memory-based self-improving agent methods (EMR and MAPO) to stochasticity and task order (addressing RQ1), (2) a detailed manual analysis of agent memory banks using a reproducible rubric (addressing RQ2), (3) an investigation of the impact of task and environment specification on agent reliability (addressing RQ3), and (4) concrete recommendations for evaluation protocols and system design to mitigate observed fragility (addressing RQ4). Each research question is directly mapped to a corresponding contribution and is discussed in the relevant results and discussion sections. Unlike prior work, which typically reports single-run results on fixed task orders, we explicitly quantify variance, task order sensitivity, and the effects of underspecification, providing new insights into the reliability of these agents.\n\n**Background**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Replay (EMR), where agents store and retrieve past experiences to inform future actions, and (b) Memory-Augmented Policy Optimization (MAPO), where agents maintain a memory of successful trajectories to guide learning. These methods have been widely adopted in recent self-improving agent literature, but their robustness to evaluation protocol choices and environment specification has not been systematically studied.\n\n**Experimental Environments**\n\nExperiments are conducted in two complex simulated environments designed to be representative of real-world sequential decision-making:\n\n1. **Multi-step Navigation Task:** This environment consists of a 10x10 grid world with randomly placed obstacles, variable start and goal locations, and optional collectible items. Each of the 20 tasks varies in obstacle density, required path length, and the presence of distractor items, with some tasks requiring detours or multi-goal navigation. The environment is stochastic: obstacle layouts and item placements are randomized per episode. This setting is representative of robotic navigation and logistics planning, where agents must generalize across diverse spatial configurations.\n\n2. **Compositional Instruction-Following Benchmark:** This environment presents agents with natural language instructions composed of multiple subgoals (e.g., \"Pick up the red key, unlock the blue door, and place the key on the table\"). Each of the 20 tasks varies in instruction complexity (2\u20135 subgoals), object types, and required action sequences. The environment includes distractor objects and ambiguous cues, requiring agents to parse and execute compositional instructions. This benchmark reflects challenges in language-conditioned control and household robotics.\n\nThese environments were selected for their diversity, complexity, and relevance to real-world applications, providing a challenging testbed for evaluating agent robustness and memory reliability.\n\n**Methods**\n\n*Experimental Setup*\n\nAgents are implemented using standard architectures: for EMR, a recurrent neural network (GRU, 2 layers, 128 hidden units) with a memory buffer of 500 entries; for MAPO, a policy network (2-layer MLP, 128 units per layer) with a memory of top-100 successful trajectories. Optimization uses Adam (learning rate 1e-3, batch size 32). Hyperparameters are selected via grid search on held-out tasks. Full implementation details, including code, random seeds, and environment generation scripts, are provided in the supplementary material. Supplementary Table 1 summarizes all hyperparameters and environment configurations.\n\n*Evaluation Protocols*\n\nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs (10 independent seeds per configuration) to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Task shuffling is implemented by generating a new random permutation of the 20 tasks for each run, ensuring that each agent experiences a unique curriculum. For each agent-method pair, we report mean and standard deviation of success rates across runs and task orderings, as well as 95% confidence intervals computed via bootstrapping (10,000 resamples). For statistical significance testing (e.g., comparing performance under default vs. shuffled task order), we use paired t-tests, assuming approximate normality of success rates across runs. Where multiple comparisons are made (e.g., across agent types and environments), we apply Bonferroni correction to control the family-wise error rate. All statistical methods and their assumptions are detailed in the supplementary material.\n\n*Manual Memory Analysis*\n\nTo better understand the sources of fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory banks after training. The analysis uses a rubric with the following detailed criteria, selected to capture key aspects of memory quality that impact agent performance:\n\n(i) **Completeness of task representation:** Does the memory entry specify all relevant task constraints and goals? For example, in the navigation task, a complete entry should include the start location, goal location, and any obstacles encountered. An incomplete entry might omit the goal or misstate the path taken. This criterion is motivated by the need for accurate recall and transfer to similar tasks.\n\n(ii) **Relevance of retrieved memories to current tasks:** Is the retrieved memory directly applicable to the current task, or is it unrelated? For instance, if the current task is to navigate from A to B avoiding obstacle X, a relevant memory would involve a similar route or obstacle, while an irrelevant memory might pertain to a different start/goal pair or lack obstacles. This criterion assesses the utility of memory retrieval for generalization.\n\n(iii) **Consistency of memory updates:** Are updates made after each episode, and do they accurately reflect new experiences? For example, if an agent completes a task with a novel strategy, the memory should be updated to include this trajectory; inconsistent updates might skip episodes or incorrectly summarize outcomes. This criterion ensures that the memory bank evolves to reflect the agent's learning.\n\nTwo annotators independently coded 50 randomly sampled memory entries per agent, using the rubric above. Disagreements were resolved through structured discussion: annotators reviewed each disputed entry together, referenced the rubric definitions, and reached consensus on the final label. This process was chosen to maximize reliability and transparency. Inter-annotator agreement (Cohen\u2019s kappa) averaged 0.81, indicating substantial agreement.\n\n*Ablation Study on Task and Environment Specification*\n\nTo systematically test the impact of task and environment underspecification, we conducted an ablation in which we incorporated additional information into the memory construction process. Specifically, we provided agents with detailed rubrics specifying explicit success criteria (e.g., for navigation: \u201creach goal location without entering forbidden zones and collect all required items\u201d) and richer environment feedback (e.g., textual feedback indicating which constraints were satisfied or violated after each episode). This information was operationalized by augmenting the memory entries with structured fields for each criterion and by providing agents with environment feedback logs during memory updates. The goal was to assess whether more explicit task and environment specification could reduce fragility. Full ablation results, including breakdowns by environment and agent type, are provided in Table 2 of the supplementary material.\n\n**Results**\n\nOur experiments yield two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods, directly addressing RQ1 and RQ2. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: for example, the standard deviation of success rates across runs reaches up to 18% for EMR and 15% for MAPO in the navigation environment (95% CI for EMR: [62%, 80%]; MAPO: [65%, 78%]; see Supplementary Table 1 for full statistics). For context, these variances are substantially higher than those reported in prior work on single-task or fixed-order evaluations, where standard deviations are typically below 5%. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, with some runs failing to improve at all (in 3/10 EMR runs, final success rate was not significantly higher than initial, p > 0.05, paired t-test with Bonferroni correction).\n\nSecond, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. When tasks are shuffled, average performance drops by 12\u201320% compared to the default curriculum order (EMR: mean drop 16%, 95% CI [12%, 20%], p < 0.01; MAPO: mean drop 14%, 95% CI [10%, 18%], p < 0.01), and in some cases, agents fail to solve later tasks entirely. This performance drop is large relative to baseline variability and highlights a key limitation of current evaluation practices.\n\nManual examination of memory banks (addressing RQ2) reveals that agents often store incomplete or ambiguous task representations (e.g., 38% of EMR entries omitted key task constraints, such as goal location or required actions), and retrieval frequently surfaces irrelevant or outdated experiences (27% of retrievals in MAPO were rated as not relevant to the current task, such as retrieving a trajectory for a different task or with outdated environment information). \n\nIn the ablation study (addressing RQ3), incorporating additional task and environment specification partially closes the performance gap: for example, the average success rate under shuffled task order improves by 7% for EMR (95% CI [4%, 10%], p < 0.05) and 6% for MAPO (95% CI [3%, 9%], p < 0"}}